# ================================================================== PART 3 · THE CORE
flow(id="megabanks", part=2, scope="world",
 title="The Megabanks: Profit Kept, Losses Shared",
 kick="A bank that cannot be allowed to fail borrows cheaply, bets heavily, and hands the bill to the public when the bets go wrong.",
 sum="Part 2 ended with a list of 29 banks that governments will not allow to collapse. This chapter shows what that promise is worth to the banks and what it has cost everyone else. The logic is short. If lenders know that a bank will be rescued, lending to it is safe. So the bank borrows more cheaply than its rivals. Cheap borrowing makes leverage more profitable, so the bank takes more risk. In good years the gains go to shareholders and staff. In a bad year the rescue arrives from the central bank and the treasury.<br><br>The crash of 2008 is the measured case. It was investigated under oath by a commission of the United States Congress, and its findings are the main source here. The chapter then shows what happens when one of these banks breaks the criminal law.",
 bridge="The first slide puts a yearly figure on the promise of rescue. The second gives the official findings on 2008: how much the banks had borrowed, and what households lost. The third is the case in which prosecutors decided a bank was too large to charge.",
 out="The promise of rescue was worth up to 590 billion dollars a year to the largest banks, the crash it encouraged destroyed nearly 11 trillion dollars of American household wealth, and a bank that laundered drug money was spared prosecution because of its size. The next chapter looks at the half of finance that is not inside banks at all.",
 rec="The largest banks keep the gains from leverage and pass the losses to the public, and the largest of them have been treated as too big to prosecute.",
 roles=["Where the findings were made", "Home of the bank in the example"],
 src=[{"t": "International Monetary Fund, Big Banks Benefit From Government Subsidies (31 March 2014)", "u": "https://www.imf.org/en/News/Articles/2015/09/28/04/53/sopol033114a"},
      {"t": "Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission, Conclusions (2011)", "u": FCIC},
      {"t": "United States Department of Justice, press release of 11 December 2012 on HSBC", "u": "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/2012/December/12-crm-1478.html"}],
 steps=[
  st("2012", "Washington", WASH, 2.2, "The Free Insurance Worth Billions a Year",
   "The International Monetary Fund set out to measure what the promise of rescue is worth. Its method was to compare what the largest banks pay to borrow with what they would pay if lenders believed they could fail. The gap is a subsidy. Nobody votes it and it appears in no budget, which is why the Fund calls it implicit. For 2012 the Fund estimated the subsidy to the largest banks at up to 70 billion dollars in the United States and up to 300 billion in the euro area. Worldwide, press reports of the study put the figure as high as 590 billion dollars in that one year. The Fund found that reforms since the crash had reduced the subsidy but that it remained large. It also named the side effects. The subsidy tilts competition against smaller banks, and it encourages the large ones to take more risk, since someone else stands behind the losses. The next slide shows what that risk-taking had already done.",
   "https://www.imf.org/en/News/Articles/2015/09/28/04/53/sopol033114a", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["imf", "jpm", "citi", "bofa"], ex=["imf_subsidy"],
   note="Shaded: the United States, where the Fund published the study. Ringed circles: the Fund and three of the banks on the list of 29, shown as examples; the study covered all the largest banks."),
  st("2008", "New York and Washington", [-75.5, 39.8], 3.0, "2008: What Borrowed Bets Did",
   "The commission appointed by Congress took evidence for more than a year and reported in January 2011. Its first conclusion was that the crisis was avoidable. It was caused by what people did and failed to do, and the commission said so in those terms. It found that the debt of the American financial sector had grown from 3 trillion dollars in 1978 to 36 trillion in 2007. The largest investment banks were leveraged as high as 40 to 1. They had borrowed that money to hold bonds built from home loans that could not be repaid, graded AAA by the agencies in the last chapter. When the bonds fell, the cushion was gone within months. The government and the central bank then rescued the system. The households did not get a rescue on the same terms. The commission counted nearly 11 trillion dollars of household wealth wiped out, 26 million people without work or enough work, and about four million families who had lost their homes.",
   FCIC, add={"United States": 0}, pl=["fcic", "fed"], ex=["fcic2011"]),
  st("2012", "London, Washington and Mexico City", [-45, 35], 1.9, "Regional Example: The Bank Too Big to Charge",
   "Regional Example: Size protects a bank in court as well. HSBC, based in London, is one of the 29. In December 2012 the United States Department of Justice announced that the bank had admitted failing to guard against money laundering. Two drug cartels, Mexico's Sinaloa cartel and Colombia's Norte del Valle cartel, had moved 881 million dollars through it. The bank paid 1.92 billion dollars, which was about six weeks of its profit the year before. No one was put on trial. The bank itself was not convicted either. It signed what is called a deferred prosecution agreement, in which charges are filed and then dropped if the firm behaves for a set period. In 2016 a committee of the House of Representatives published internal papers on the decision. They showed that the department's own money-laundering section had recommended prosecuting, and that its leaders overruled the section out of fear of what a conviction might do to the financial system.",
   "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/2012/December/12-crm-1478.html", add={"United States": 0, "United Kingdom": 1, "Mexico": 1}, arcs=[[MEXICO, NY], [LONDON, WASH]], an=[["Mexico City", "New York"], ["London", "Washington"]],
   pl=["hsbc", "doj"], ex=["hsbc2012"], reg=True),
 ])

flow(id="shadow", part=2, scope="world",
 title="The Shadow Banks: Half of Finance Outside the Bank Rules",
 kick="Funds and lenders that are not banks now hold more than half of the world's financial assets.",
 sum="After 2008 the rules for banks were tightened a little, as the Basel chapter showed. Money moved to where the rules were lighter. A large and growing share of lending and investing is now done by firms that are not banks: investment funds, hedge funds, money market funds, insurers, pension funds and private lenders. Officials call this sector nonbank financial intermediation. Its older name is shadow banking.<br><br>These firms do many of the things banks do. They take in money that people expect to get back quickly and put it into things that cannot be sold quickly. But they do not hold a bank's cushion, they are not supervised like a bank, and in several areas the authorities admit they cannot see what is going on. This chapter gives the size of the sector and explains why its risks end up with the public anyway.",
 bridge="The first slide gives the official measure of the sector. The second explains how a fund that is not a bank can still suffer a run, and who has stepped in when that happened.",
 out="Firms outside the banking rules hold 256.8 trillion dollars, which is 51 per cent of the world's financial assets, and the body that monitors them reports severe gaps in what it can see. The next part asks who owns the companies that all this money is invested in.",
 rec="More than half of the world's financial assets now sit with firms outside the rules written for banks.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "Financial Stability Board, Global Monitoring Report on Nonbank Financial Intermediation 2025 (16 December 2025)", "u": FSBN},
      {"t": "Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, The Fed's Dollar Liquidity Swap Lines (2024)", "u": "https://www.richmondfed.org/publications/research/econ_focus/2024/q4_federal_reserve"}],
 steps=[
  st("2024", "Twenty-nine jurisdictions", WORLD, 1.15, "More Than Half of All Financial Assets",
   "The Financial Stability Board counts the sector every year across 29 jurisdictions that produce over 90 per cent of the world's output. Its report of December 2025 covers the year 2024. In that year the nonbank sector grew by 9.4 per cent, twice as fast as the banks. It reached 256.8 trillion dollars, which is 51 per cent of all the financial assets in those jurisdictions. Banks, which are what most people think of as finance, are now the smaller half. Inside the total, the Board picks out the firms that lend in ways that could cause the same kind of trouble as a failing bank. That narrower group grew by 12 per cent to 76.3 trillion dollars. The Board then makes an admission. It reports severe limits on the data available for private credit, which is lending by funds directly to companies. So one of the fastest-growing kinds of lending is one that the world's financial watchdog says it cannot properly measure.",
   FSBN, w=1, pl=["fsb"], ex=["fsb_nbfi"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the report gives one total for its 29 jurisdictions together."),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "The Same Run, Without the Same Rules",
   "The mechanics chapter explained a bank run: money promised on demand, tied up in things that take time to sell. A fund can be in the same position. Investors in a money market fund expect to withdraw at any time. A fund that lends to companies holds loans that may take years to be repaid. If enough investors ask for their money together, the fund must sell what it holds at once, prices fall, and other funds holding the same things are dragged down. In March 2020 that kind of scramble for cash spread through the markets within days, and it was one reason the Federal Reserve had 449 billion dollars out on its swap lines by May. So the pattern from the megabanks repeats one step further out. The firms sit outside the bank rules and keep the profit that lighter rules allow. When a panic starts, the central bank stops it, because the alternative is a general collapse. The risk was private until the day it was not.",
   "https://www.richmondfed.org/publications/research/econ_focus/2024/q4_federal_reserve", w=1, pl=["fsb", "fed"], ex=["fsb_nbfi", "swap2020"],
   note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. It explains a mechanism that applies wherever such funds operate."),
 ])

# ================================================================== PART 4 · THE OWNERS
flow(id="bigthree", part=3, scope="world",
 title="The Big Three: One Shareholder Behind Almost Every Large Company",
 kick="Three fund managers, investing other people's savings, have become the largest shareholder in most big American companies.",
 sum="The mechanics chapter said that a company is run for its shareholders, and that the shareholders elect the board. So it matters a great deal who the shareholders are. The answer has changed in one generation. Most shares are no longer held directly by individuals. They are held through funds, and the fund manager casts the votes.<br><br>The most popular kind is the index fund, which simply buys a slice of every company on a list. It is cheap, so pension schemes and ordinary savers have poured into it. Three firms dominate that business: BlackRock, Vanguard and State Street. The savings belong to millions of people. The votes that come with the shares are cast by three offices. This chapter gives the size of the largest of them and the reach of the three together.",
 bridge="The first slide shows how much money the largest manager controls. The second gives the research finding on how many big companies have the three as their largest shareholder, and how they vote.",
 out="BlackRock alone manages 14 trillion dollars, and the three managers together are the largest shareholder in 88 per cent of the biggest American companies, where they usually vote with management. The next chapter asks whose savings these are, and what the companies do for their owners.",
 rec="Three fund managers cast the shareholder votes in most large companies, using savings that belong to other people.",
 roles=["Home of the three managers"],
 src=[{"t": "Reuters, BlackRock's assets hit record 14 trillion dollars (15 January 2026)", "u": "https://www.bnnbloomberg.ca/business/2026/01/15/blackrocks-assets-hit-record-us14-trillion-on-fourth-quarter-markets-rally/"},
      {"t": "J. Fichtner, E. Heemskerk and J. Garcia-Bernardo, Hidden Power of the Big Three? (Business and Politics, 2017)", "u": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/30AD689509AAD62F5B677E916C28C4B6"}],
 steps=[
  st("End of 2025", "New York", NY, 3.0, "Fourteen Trillion Dollars in One Firm's Hands",
   "BlackRock, based in New York, is the largest fund manager in the world. At the end of 2025 it managed 14.04 trillion dollars, up from 11.55 trillion a year earlier. For comparison, the foreign-currency reserves of all the world's central banks together come to about 13 trillion dollars. The money is not BlackRock's. It belongs to pension schemes, insurers, governments and individual savers who have bought its funds. BlackRock earns a fee on it and, for the shares, exercises the vote. Most of the growth is in index funds. An index fund does not choose companies. It buys every company on a list in proportion to size. That makes it cheap, and it also means the fund never sells a company because it is badly run or pays badly. It holds everything, permanently. A saver who puts a pension into such a fund becomes a part-owner of thousands of companies and hands the voice that goes with ownership to the manager. The next slide shows what that adds up to.",
   "https://www.bnnbloomberg.ca/business/2026/01/15/blackrocks-assets-hit-record-us14-trillion-on-fourth-quarter-markets-rally/", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["blackrock", "vanguard", "statestreet"], ex=["blackrock14"]),
  st("2017", "The United States", [-96, 39], 2.7, "The Largest Shareholder in 88 Per Cent of Big Companies",
   "Three researchers at the University of Amsterdam mapped the holdings of BlackRock, Vanguard and State Street across every company listed in the United States. Their study was published in 2017. Taken together, the three were the largest shareholder in 88 per cent of the companies in the S&P 500, the list of America's biggest firms. Across all listed American companies they were the largest shareholder in 40 per cent. The researchers then examined how the three voted at shareholder meetings. Each firm voted its many funds as a block, from one central office, and they generally voted with the company's management. Think about what follows. The same three owners sit behind competing airlines, competing banks and competing drug makers. An owner of every firm in an industry gains nothing when one of them cuts prices to take business from another. It gains when the whole industry's profits rise. No law was broken to reach this position. It was built from ordinary people's pension savings.",
   "https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/30AD689509AAD62F5B677E916C28C4B6", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["blackrock", "vanguard", "statestreet"], ex=["bigthree88"]),
 ])

flow(id="shareholders", part=3, scope="world",
 title="Who Owns the Shares, and What Companies Do for Them",
 kick="Share ownership is concentrated in the richest tenth of households, and large companies now return over a trillion dollars a year to them.",
 sum="It is often said that everyone is a shareholder now, through a pension or a savings plan. In the country with the largest stock market that is true only in the thinnest sense. Most households own a little. A small group owns nearly all of it. This matters because of the rule from the mechanics chapter: whatever a company saves on wages, tax or investment is left over for its shareholders.<br><br>This chapter sets out who holds the shares in the United States, using the central bank's own figures. It then shows how much the largest companies hand to shareholders each year, and how. It ends with a map of where the world's stock markets are, because in most countries a stock market of any size does not exist and the question of ownership is settled abroad.",
 bridge="The first slide gives the split of share ownership between the richest tenth and everyone else. The second shows the yearly sum that large companies pay out to shareholders. The third maps the size of each country's stock market.",
 out="The richest tenth of American households own about 87 per cent of the shares held by households, and the 500 largest companies returned a record 1.685 trillion dollars to shareholders in a year. The next chapter turns to a kind of owner that buys whole companies with borrowed money.",
 rec="Shares are held overwhelmingly by the richest tenth, and companies pay out record sums to them.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "Federal Reserve, Distributional Financial Accounts", "u": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/z1/dataviz/dfa"},
      {"t": "Axios, Stocks drive record share of American wealth (4 June 2026)", "u": "https://www.axios.com/2026/06/04/stocks-ai-wealth-households"},
      {"t": "S&P Dow Jones Indices, S&P 500 buybacks, third quarter 2025", "u": SPB},
      {"t": "World Bank, Market capitalization of listed domestic companies (per cent of GDP)", "u": WBDATA + "CM.MKT.LCAP.GD.ZS"}],
 steps=[
  st("End of 2025", "The United States", [-96, 39], 2.7, "Regional Example: The Top Tenth Owns Almost All of It",
   "Regional Example: The Federal Reserve publishes a quarterly count of who owns what in the United States. At the end of 2025 the richest 10 per cent of households owned about 87 per cent of all the shares held by households. Two years earlier the figure had reached a record 93 per cent. The poorer half of the country owned about 1 per cent. A majority of American households do own some shares, mostly through retirement accounts, so the claim that ordinary people are in the market is true. The amounts are what differ. When share prices rise by a trillion dollars, roughly 870 billion goes to the top tenth and about 10 billion is spread across the bottom half. The same count shows where this leads. In the third quarter of 2025 the richest 1 per cent held 31.7 per cent of all household wealth, the highest share since the series began in 1989. So a policy that lifts share prices is, in practice, a policy for that tenth.",
   "https://www.axios.com/2026/06/04/stocks-ai-wealth-households", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["fed"], ex=["dfa2025"], reg=True),
  st("Year to September 2025", "New York", NY, 3.0, "A Trillion Dollars Spent Buying Back Shares",
   "The mechanics chapter described the buyback: a company spends its cash buying its own shares, which raises the price for the owners who remain. S&P Dow Jones Indices, the firm that keeps the list of America's 500 largest companies, adds up what they spend. In the twelve months to September 2025, those companies spent a record 1.020 trillion dollars on buybacks. With dividends added, they returned a record 1.685 trillion dollars to shareholders in one year. Twenty companies accounted for about half of the buybacks. Put this next to the last slide. Money spent on a buyback is money not spent on wages, on new plant, on research or on lower prices. It goes to whoever holds the shares, and seven-eighths of the shares held by households are held by the richest tenth. Managers favour buybacks for a further reason. Their own pay is usually tied to the share price. So the company's cash, the managers' interest and the owners' interest all point the same way, and the workforce is not part of it.",
   SPB, add={"United States": 0}, pl=["spdji"], ex=["buybacks2025"]),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "Where the Stock Markets Are",
   "The World Bank compares the total value of the companies listed on each country's stock exchange with the size of the country's economy. The map shades the %(mcap_n)s countries that have reported a figure since 2018. In %(mcap_hi)s of them, the listed companies are worth more than a full year of the country's output. The United States, at about 224 per cent, is among them, and so are Switzerland, India, Canada and South Africa. Most of the world is either lightly shaded or left clear. In those countries there is little or no stock market. Their large employers are owned from somewhere else: by a parent company listed in New York, London or Hong Kong, or by a fund. That is how the ownership pattern of one country becomes a fact of life in many. A mine or a factory or a phone network may operate in a country with no stock exchange, while the decision about how much of its earnings stay there is made for shareholders on another continent.",
   WBDATA + "CM.MKT.LCAP.GD.ZS", data={"k": "mcap", "only": None}, pl=["worldbank"]),
 ])

flow(id="pe", part=3, scope="na",
 title="Private Equity: Buying a Company With Its Own Debt",
 kick="A private equity firm buys a company mostly with borrowed money, and the debt is placed on the company that was bought.",
 sum="Index funds own a slice of everything and do nothing. A second kind of owner does the opposite. A private equity firm raises a fund from pension schemes and wealthy investors, uses it to buy whole companies, runs them for a few years and sells them. The firms describe this as improving businesses. The method deserves a closer look, because it applies leverage, the tool from the mechanics chapter, to a company that employs people.<br><br>The purchase is called a leveraged buyout. Most of the price is borrowed, and the loan becomes a debt of the company that was bought. The company's earnings then have to cover interest that did not exist the day before. This chapter walks through the arithmetic and then gives the record of what happened to such companies in one year.",
 bridge="The first slide works through a buyout with round numbers, as an if and a then. The second gives the count of large bankruptcies in the United States in 2024 and how many had a private equity owner.",
 out="In a leveraged buyout the company carries the debt and the buyer keeps the gain. In 2024, companies with a private equity owner in their history made up 56 per cent of large American bankruptcies. That ends the account of the owners. Part 5 follows the money they make out of reach of the tax office.",
 rec="Private equity buys companies with debt that the companies themselves must repay, and those companies fail far more often.",
 roles=["Where the bankruptcies were counted"],
 src=[{"t": "Private Equity Stakeholder Project, Private equity industry behind over half of large US bankruptcies in 2024 (12 February 2025)", "u": PESP}],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The United States", [-96, 39], 2.7, "Regional Example: How a Leveraged Buyout Works",
   "Regional Example: Take a chain of care homes worth 100 million. A private equity fund puts in 30 million of its investors' money and borrows 70 million. It buys the chain. The 70 million is then recorded as a debt of the chain itself. Before the sale the chain owed nothing. Now it owes 70 million and must pay the interest out of what residents pay. Follow the two outcomes. If things go well, the fund sells the chain after five years for 130 million, repays the 70 million and keeps 60 million on its 30 million. If things go badly, the chain cannot cover the interest and goes bankrupt. The fund loses at most its 30 million, and may already have taken that back in fees and special dividends. The staff lose their jobs and the residents lose their home. This is the leverage rule from the mechanics chapter, with one change. The gain goes to the buyer. The risk of the borrowing sits with the company, its workers and its customers.",
   PESP, add={"United States": 0}, pl=["blackstone", "kkr", "apollo", "carlyle"], reg=True,
   note="Shaded: the United States. The figures in this slide are a worked example with round numbers and describe no particular company. Ringed circles: four of the largest private equity firms, shown so that the kind of firm can be located; the example is not about any one of them."),
  st("2024", "The United States", [-96, 39], 2.7, "Regional Example: More Than Half of the Large Bankruptcies",
   "Regional Example: The Private Equity Stakeholder Project, a research group that follows the industry, examined every large corporate bankruptcy in the United States in 2024. It defined large as debts of more than 500 million dollars. Of those, 56 per cent involved companies with a history of private equity ownership. Across bankruptcies of all sizes, companies owned by private equity made up 11 per cent, 75 filings out of 697. The industry's own lobby says private equity accounts for 6.5 per cent of the economy, so its companies were failing at well above their share. The group counted at least 65,850 jobs lost in those bankruptcies. Health care stood out. Seven of the eight largest health care bankruptcies of the year involved companies that private equity had owned. This is what the last slide predicts. A company loaded with debt to pay for its own purchase has less room when costs or interest rates rise. The pension schemes that supplied the fund's money are often the schemes of workers in similar jobs.",
   PESP, add={"United States": 0}, pl=["pesp"], ex=["pesp2024"], reg=True),
 ])

# ================================================================== PART 5 · THE EXITS
flow(id="havens", part=4, scope="world",
 title="Tax Havens: Profit Booked Somewhere Else",
 kick="Multinational companies record a third of their foreign profit in places where little business is done and little tax is charged.",
 sum="Parts 3 and 4 showed where profit is made and who owns it. This part follows it out of the door. A company that operates in fifty countries does not have to report its profit in the countries where it earned it. With help from banks, law firms and accountants, it can arrange for the profit to appear in a place that charges almost no tax. Such places are called tax havens, or secrecy jurisdictions when what they sell is concealment of who owns what.<br><br>This is the financial system at work, since every step needs a bank account, a company registration and a professional signature. It is also where the story reaches the public purse most directly. Tax that is not paid by companies and the very wealthy is either paid by someone else or shows up as a hospital that is not built. The chapter explains the method, gives the best estimates of the sums, and maps how much secrecy each country sells.",
 bridge="The first slide shows how profit is moved on paper. The second gives the yearly tax lost and names the governments that voted against writing new rules at the United Nations. The third measures wealth held offshore by households. The fourth maps the Financial Secrecy Index.",
 out="A trillion dollars of profit a year is shifted to havens, countries lose an estimated 492 billion dollars in tax, and household wealth equal to a tenth of the world's output sits offshore. The next chapter shows what this means for one continent.",
 rec="Profit is booked where tax is lowest, at a cost to other countries of about half a trillion dollars a year.",
 roles=["Voted against the terms for a UN tax convention, August 2024"],
 src=[{"t": "EU Tax Observatory, Global Tax Evasion Report 2024, executive summary", "u": EUTAX},
      {"t": "Tax Justice Network, The State of Tax Justice 2024", "u": TJN},
      {"t": "Tax Justice Network, Financial Secrecy Index 2025", "u": "https://fsi.taxjustice.net"}],
 steps=[
  st("2022", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "How Profit Moves Without Anything Moving",
   "A multinational is a group of companies under one owner. The parts trade with each other, and the group sets the prices. That is the opening. Suppose the group sells coffee in a country with a 30 per cent tax on profit. It sets up a subsidiary on an island that charges almost nothing and gives that subsidiary the ownership of the brand name. The coffee shops then pay the island company a large yearly fee for using the name. On paper, the shops barely make a profit, and the island company, which may have no staff, makes a great deal. No coffee and no people have moved. The EU Tax Observatory, a research centre in Paris, measures the result. In 2022 multinationals shifted about one trillion dollars of profit to tax havens. That was 35 per cent of all the profit they made outside their home countries. The Observatory adds that years of promised reform have made little difference to that share. A local firm with one address has no such option and pays the full rate.",
   EUTAX, w=1, pl=["eutax"], ex=["eutax2024"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. The source gives a world total. The fourth slide of this chapter maps secrecy country by country."),
  st("2024", "Eight governments", WORLD, 1.15, "Half a Trillion Dollars of Tax Lost Each Year",
   "The Tax Justice Network, a research group based in Britain, estimates what this costs governments. Its 2024 report puts the loss at 492 billion dollars a year. About 348 billion of that is lost to multinational companies shifting profit. About 145 billion is lost to wealthy individuals holding assets offshore. The report then looks at who stands in the way of change. In August 2024, countries voted at the United Nations on the terms for a worldwide tax convention. Eight voted against: the United States, the United Kingdom, Australia, Canada, Japan, South Korea, Israel and New Zealand. The Network calculates that these eight enable about 43 per cent of the losses, through their own rules and the havens linked to them. It also finds that they lose heavily themselves. The United States loses about 73 billion dollars a year and the United Kingdom about 45 billion. So the governments are not defending their own treasuries. They are defending the financial centres and the companies that do the shifting.",
   TJN, w=1, add={"United States": 0, "United Kingdom": 0, "Australia": 0, "Canada": 0, "Japan": 0, "Korea, Rep.": 0, "Israel": 0, "New Zealand": 0}, pl=["tjn"], ex=["tjn2024"],
   note="Shaded: the eight countries that the source says voted against the terms in August 2024. The estimate of tax lost is for the whole world."),
  st("2022", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "A Tenth of the World's Output, Held Offshore",
   "Companies shift profit. Wealthy households move the wealth itself. They hold bank accounts, shares and bonds through banks and shell companies in other countries. A shell company is one that exists only on paper, to hold assets under a name that is not the owner's. The EU Tax Observatory estimates that the financial wealth households hold offshore equals about 10 per cent of the world's yearly output. There has been real progress here. Since 2017, banks in most countries have had to report foreign customers' accounts to the customers' home tax offices. The Observatory finds that this cut offshore tax evasion to about a third of what it was. It also finds that about a quarter of offshore financial wealth still goes untaxed. Two lessons follow. Secrecy was a choice all along, since one change of rule removed much of it. And a quarter of a sum that large is still a great deal of untaxed wealth, held by the people best able to pay.",
   EUTAX, w=1, pl=["eutax"], ex=["eutax2024"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the source gives world totals."),
  st("2025", "Every place in the index", WORLD, 1.15, "How Much Secrecy Each Country Sells",
   "Which places supply the secrecy? The Tax Justice Network scores %(fsi_n)s jurisdictions for its Financial Secrecy Index. The secrecy score runs from 0 to 100. It is built from the country's own laws: whether the real owners of companies are recorded and published, whether banks must report to foreign tax offices, whether trusts can hide who benefits. A higher score means more can be hidden. The map shades each country by that score, and %(fsi_hi)s of the countries shown score 70 or more. A small island with a high score may handle very little money, so the Network also weighs each score by how much foreign business the place does, and ranks the results. In 2025 the United States ranks first, Switzerland second and Singapore third. The largest suppliers of financial secrecy are large, rich countries with big financial centres. They demand information about their own citizens' accounts abroad and are slower to hand over the same about foreigners' accounts at home. This is the map to hold next to the vote on the last slide. A government whose banks earn fees by keeping the rest of the world's wealth out of sight has a reason to leave the rules as they are.",
   "https://fsi.taxjustice.net", data={"k": "fsi", "only": None}, pl=["tjn"]),
 ])

flow(id="africa", part=4, scope="af",
 title="Africa: More Money Leaves Than Arrives",
 kick="The continent that is described as living on aid sends more abroad through hidden channels than it receives.",
 sum="The last chapter gave world totals. This one shows what the same system does to a single region, using a study by the United Nations. Africa is usually discussed as a recipient: of aid, of loans, of investment. The study added up the money going the other way that does not appear in official accounts. It includes profit shifted by companies, exports recorded at less than their true value so that the difference can be collected abroad, and the proceeds of corruption and crime moved to foreign banks.<br><br>The technical name for all of this is illicit financial flows. Every dollar of it passes through the banks, company registries and financial centres described in earlier chapters. The chapter is a regional example, and it is here because it shows the whole mechanism in one place.",
 bridge="The first slide compares what leaves Africa each year with what arrives as aid and investment. The second shows where the largest part of it comes from and what it costs in public services.",
 out="About 88.6 billion dollars a year leaves Africa through hidden channels, more than it receives in aid, and over fifteen years the total exceeded everything the continent owes abroad. That leads to the next part: the debts themselves.",
 rec="Africa loses more each year through hidden outflows than it receives in aid, and over time more than its entire foreign debt.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "UNCTAD, Economic Development in Africa Report 2020, press release of 28 September 2020", "u": UNAF},
      {"t": "UN News, Illicit capital robbing Africa and its people of their future (28 September 2020)", "u": "https://news.un.org/en/story/2020/09/1074052"}],
 steps=[
  st("2013 to 2015", "Africa", [20, 2], 2.3, "Regional Example: 88.6 Billion Dollars Out, Every Year",
   "Regional Example: UN Trade and Development published its count in September 2020. It estimated that 88.6 billion dollars leaves Africa every year as illicit capital flight. That equals 3.7 per cent of everything the continent produces. Compare it with what comes in. Over the same years Africa received about 48 billion dollars a year in aid and about 54 billion in foreign investment. So the hidden outflow was nearly as large as the two best-known inflows added together, and well above aid alone. The report then made a longer comparison. Between 2000 and 2015 the outflow came to 836 billion dollars. Africa's entire foreign debt in 2018 was 770 billion. On that count, the report said, Africa is a net creditor to the world. The rest of the world owes Africa more than Africa owes it. The picture of a continent kept going by outside generosity has the direction of the money backwards. What it lacks is the tax and the earnings that leave before they are counted.",
   UNAF, pl=["unctad"], ex=["africa2020"], reg=True,
   note="No country is shaded, because the source gives one figure for the continent. The ringed circle is UN Trade and Development in Geneva, which published it."),
  st("2015", "Africa", [20, 2], 2.3, "Regional Example: Gold, Mispriced Exports and Missing Clinics",
   "Regional Example: The same report traced where the outflow starts. The largest single part, about 40 billion dollars in 2015, was tied to exports of minerals and other raw materials. Of that, 77 per cent came from gold, 12 per cent from diamonds and 6 per cent from platinum. The usual method is mispricing. A shipment is declared at the border for less than it is worth. The buyer abroad pays the true price, and the difference stays in a foreign account. The exporting country collects tax only on the lower figure. The report estimated this practice alone at 30 to 52 billion dollars a year. It then measured the damage. African countries with high illicit outflows spent 25 per cent less on health and 58 per cent less on education than countries with low ones. Each step needed a trading company, a bank and a haven of the kind mapped in the last chapter. The gold is real and it leaves in the open. The money it earned is what goes missing.",
   "https://news.un.org/en/story/2020/09/1074052", pl=["unctad"], ex=["africa2020"], reg=True,
   note="No country is shaded, because the source gives figures for the continent as a whole."),
 ])
